  "problem": "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $n>2,$ then $______.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is trivial?\n\n- $n=0$\n- $0>1$\n- ${n}^{2}\\ge 0$",
  "worked_solution": "We say that an implication has a *trivial proof* if the consequent is always true:\n\n$$\nP\\,\u21d2\\overset{\\text{Always true}}{\\overset{\u23de}{Q}}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the antecedent is true or false.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our consequents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always true. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always true. $\\,\u2713$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
